  "query_preview": "..Most activity on-going at the moment is across \nnorthern Apache County and areas south of I-40. The complex of \nshowers and storms is from a MCS that formed across central \nArizona earlier this evening. We anticipate these showers/storms \nto continue to push northward toward the Flagstaff area overnight,\nso you may hear a few rumbles of thunder and wake up to the smell\nof rain. If the recent run of the U of A WRF model is accurate, \nthere will be showers/thunderstorms across much of \ncentral/northern AZ through the next 24 hours. Hence, the Flash \nFlood Watch continue for our entire CWA through Monday night at \nmidnight. Although flash flooding is a primary concern, especially\nfor recent burn scar locations, storms will also be capable of \nproducing hail, frequent lightning, and gusty/erratic winds. Be \nsure to have a way to receive warnings if you're out and about. \n\nDespite hi-res guidance being fairly persistent with activity for\nlater this afternoon/evening, a lot may also depend on how worked\nover everything is after tonight. It could simply delay the start\nfor showers/thunderstorms this afternoon, but it could also keep \nthings fairly tame. A shortwave looks to clip the northwestern \ncorner of the state today, so this will also help with keeping \nmoisture funneling into our CWA and ensuring the proper dynamics \nare in place for continuous convection. In short, the ingredients\nare there for an active day, but patterns like this can be tricky\nwhen we are dealing with overnight convection. \n\nTuesday through Thursday...A large trough over the Pacific\nNorthwest with an embedded shortwave across the Great Basin region\nwill suppress monsoonal moisture through the middle of the week.\nWe aren't expecting to completely dry out, but showers/storms will\nbecome very limited over the higher terrain each afternoon.\n\nThis short break from active weather doesn't look to be long-\nlived, as more chances for showers/thunderstorms look to return\nFriday through the weekend. This return of active weather will be\nfrom high pressure strengthening across the central US and also a\ntropical disturbance forecast to move northward along the\nCalifornia Baja coast. There is still some uncertainty with the\nexact trajectory of this tropical system, but even without that\nadditional moisture, the ridge should end up in a more favorable\nmonsoon pattern.",
